arXiv:2510.01384v1 [cs.LG] 01 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for Provable
<br class="ltx_break">Self-Correction</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jaeyeon Kim
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Seunggeun Kim
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Taekyun Lee
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">David Z. Pan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>UT Austin
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hyeji Kim
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>UT Austin
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sham Kakade
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span> Harvard University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Kempner Institute

</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sitan Chen
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span> Harvard University
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">A natural desideratum for generative models is <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-correction</em>–detecting and revising low-quality tokens at inference. While Masked Diffusion Models (MDMs) have emerged as a promising approach for generative modeling in discrete spaces, their capacity for self-correction remains poorly understood. Prior attempts to incorporate self-correction into MDMs either require overhauling MDM architectures/training or rely on imprecise proxies for token quality, limiting their applicability. Motivated by this, we introduce PRISM–<span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>lug-in <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>emasking for <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I</span>nference-time <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>elf-correction of <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>asked Diffusions–a lightweight, model-agnostic approach that applies to any pretrained MDM. Theoretically, PRISM defines a self-correction loss that provably learns per-token quality scores, without RL or a verifier. These quality scores are computed in the same forward pass with MDM and used to detect low-quality tokens. Empirically, PRISM advances MDM inference across domains and scales: Sudoku; unconditional text (170M); and code with LLaDA (8B).</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="width:397.5pt;">     
<span id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="p1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></span></span>
</span>
</span></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Masked Diffusion Models (MDMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>
have emerged as a promising approach for generative modeling in discrete domains. At inference time, they start from a fully masked sequence and gradually unmask the masked tokens in arbitrary order to obtain a clean sequence. This flexibility in generation order, combined with recent approaches to deployment at scale <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepMind, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labs et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, has enabled them to surpass their autoregressive counterparts on several downstream tasks, such as reasoning, coding, and planning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, the standard MDM design lacks the ability to self-correct–<em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">a natural desideratum for generative models</em>–identifying low-quality or incorrect tokens and correcting them at inference. In MDMs, once a mask token is unmasked, it remains fixed for the entire inference (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, left). Consequently, the model cannot revise early mistakes, a limitation several recent methods have tried to overcome <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lezama et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">von Rütte et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Unfortunately, existing approaches come with one of two drawbacks: they either <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> indirectly or inaccurately learn a notion of token quality that lacks theoretical grounding and is potentially inefficient to evaluate or <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> entirely change the MDM framework or architecture, limiting their applicability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contribution.</span> In this work, we propose a simple, principled solution addressing these limitations, <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">PRISM</span>: <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">P</span>lug-in <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">R</span>emasking for <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">I</span>nference-time <span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">S</span>elf-correction of <span id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">M</span>asked diffusion models, a plug-and-play fine-tuning framework that easily adapts to any pretrained MDM (addressing <span id="S1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span>) and directly learns the clean token’s quality given a partially masked sequence (addressing <span id="S1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Theoretically</span>, PRISM introduces a novel self-correction loss that <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">provably</em> learns the per-token quality scores. It attaches a lightweight adapter to any pretrained MDM and fine-tunes with this loss; at inference, the learned scores detect low-quality tokens to <em id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">remask</em> and potentially be revised in subsequent inference steps (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, right). <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Practically</span>, PRISM improves MDM inference across a range of domains and scales: Sudoku, unconditional text generation with a 170M MDM, and code generation with LLaDA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, an open-source 8B MDM. Notably, on LLaDA, with <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;500</annotation></semantics></math> GPU-hours of fine-tuning, it acquires the ability to self-correct, outperforming baselines on MBPP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al. (2021b)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Organization.</span> We begin by reviewing MDMs in Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Masked Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> and previous work on equipping MDMs with self-correction ability in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Self-correction for MDM via per-token quality ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. We introduce PRISM in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> with its theoretical foundation and validate its empirical ability in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01384v1/PRISM_main.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/179;" width="548" height="179"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PRISM overview</span>. MDMs learn an <em id="S1.F1.11" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">unmasking posterior</em> to unmask tokens, after which they remain fixed (<span id="S1.F1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left</span>). PRISM fine-tuning introduces <em id="S1.F1.13" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;">per-token quality</em>, which is used to detect incorrect tokens and <em id="S1.F1.14" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">remask</em> them. At inference, the fine-tuned MDM jointly computes <em id="S1.F1.15" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">unmasking posterior</em> and <em id="S1.F1.16" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;">per-token quality</em>, respectively performing unmasking and remasking (<span id="S1.F1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right</span>).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Masked Diffusion Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation.</span> Assume we aim to learn the distribution over sequences of length <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> with vocabulary <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g., natural language, namely the true distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th element of a given sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}=({\mathbf{x}}^{1},\dots,{\mathbf{x}}^{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}=({\mathbf{x}}^{1},\dots,{\mathbf{x}}^{L})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the simplex of probability distributions over <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MDM training.</span> Although MDMs can be interpreted from several viewpoints, we adopt an <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">any-order</em> language model viewpoint <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which is essential to understanding our PRISM framework. Roughly speaking, MDM learns the posterior marginals over clean tokens in each masked position, conditioned on a partially masked sequence. We now formalize how this objective is defined and trained. To <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">learn</em> the posterior, MDM performs a <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">masking process</em> using an auxiliary mask token <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a given <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, this masking process samples a partially masked sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}\in(\mathcal{V}\cup\{\mathbf{m}\})^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}\in(\mathcal{V}\cup\{\mathbf{m}\})^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in two equivalent ways (see Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Marginal Equivalence of the Masking Process ‣ Appendix B Training algorithms and guarantees of PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> for equivalence):</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:20pt;">
<li id="S2.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Draw <math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\sim\mathrm{Unif}\{0,\dots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\sim\mathrm{Unif}\{0,\dots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math> and replace the tokens at uniformly selected <math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> indices in <math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Draw <math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim\mathrm{Unif}[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim\mathrm{Unif}[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and for each <math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, replace <math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}^{i}\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}^{i}\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}</annotation></semantics></math> with probability <math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">To clarify, in (1), for a given <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we uniformly sample an index set from all size-<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> subsets of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and mask corresponding positions of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This masking process defines a random variable <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and marginalizing it over <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;--ltx-bg-color:#ECF0FC;">induces a joint distribution over <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;--ltx-bg-color:#ECF0FC;" mathbackground="#ECF0FC" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;--ltx-bg-color:#ECF0FC;" mathbackground="#ECF0FC" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;--ltx-bg-color:#ECF0FC;" mathbackground="#ECF0FC" mathcolor="#4169E1">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;--ltx-bg-color:#ECF0FC;" mathbackground="#ECF0FC" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐳</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;--ltx-bg-color:#ECF0FC;" mathbackground="#ECF0FC" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We refer to the resulting coordinate-wise posterior conditioned on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;--ltx-bg-color:#ECF0FC;"><em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unmasking posterior</em></span>; <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the distribution over clean tokens at position <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> given a partially masked sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This unmasking posterior is the core modeling component in MDM and is parameterized by a neural network <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, which takes <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> as an input and outputs a tensor of shape <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}|\times L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}|\times L</annotation></semantics></math>. Its <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th column, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})\in\Delta(\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})\in\Delta(\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math>, models the unmasking posterior <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}^{i}(v\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})\approx p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=v\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}^{i}(v\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})\approx p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=v\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math>. To train <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, MDM minimizes the following loss:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)\colon=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}}}\left[\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i\colon{\mathbf{z}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}}-\log f_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{x}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)\colon=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}}}\left[\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i\colon{\mathbf{z}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}}-\log f_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{x}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which is the cross-entropy loss summed over all masked indices. Here, the expectation is taken over the joint distribution of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math> defined above. As desired, the unique minimizer <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the loss <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{\star}}^{i}(v\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=v\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{\star}}^{i}(v\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=v\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math>. This connects the MDM training loss to any-order language models such as BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, which model the posterior marginals at all masked positions. We note that the training loss equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Masked Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> allows other equivalent formulations, as developed in previous MDM works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MDM inference.</span> MDM inference starts from a length-<math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> masked sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{1}=(\mathbf{m},\dots,\mathbf{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{1}=(\mathbf{m},\dots,\mathbf{m})</annotation></semantics></math> and proceeds over a monotonically decreasing sequence of times <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}=1&gt;\dots&gt;t_{N}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}=1&gt;\dots&gt;t_{N}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. At each step <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, given partially masked sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}\in(\mathcal{V}\cup\{\mathbf{m}\})^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}\in(\mathcal{V}\cup\{\mathbf{m}\})^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, we proceed by two steps to obtain <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell+1}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:17pt;">
<li id="S2.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Choose a subset of masked tokens <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> to <span id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;--ltx-bg-color:#ECF0FC;">unmask</span>, <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\{i\,|\,{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\{i\,|\,{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;--ltx-bg-color:#ECF0FC;">unmask</span>  <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to a clean token sampled from <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}})\in\Delta(\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}})\in\Delta(\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Notably, step (a)—the choice of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>—is highly flexible and central to MDM’s gains on downstream tasks; <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> may be chosen arbitrarily. Two predominant strategies are independently including each masked index <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}</annotation></semantics></math> with a fixed probability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or adaptively selecting indices, e.g., the most confident tokens or the leftmost tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Self-correction for MDM via per-token quality</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An ideal generative model would detect low-quality tokens at inference time and correct them when appropriate. Prior work has explored self-correction in LLMs, including <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gou et al. (2023)</a></cite>, and, recently, in MDMs. Despite varying instantiations, these methods for MDM can be organized into a common two-step procedure. At each inference step, given <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ul id="S2.I3" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:17pt;">
<li id="S2.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compute a per-token “quality score” for each <math id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}\neq\mathbf{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}\neq\mathbf{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and, according to it, select a subset of clean tokens <math id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to <span id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;--ltx-bg-color:#E6F5F3;">remask</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S2.I3.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S2.I3.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;--ltx-bg-color:#E6F5F3;">remask</span>  <math id="S2.I3.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and either replace it with a new clean or leave it masked.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">At step (a), the set <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is typically chosen with the lowest per-token quality, optionally with stochasticity. At step (b), the remasked <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can be immediately resampled (using the pretrained model or an auxiliary module) or kept to engage further for future unmasking/remasking steps. Combined with the standard MDM unmasking step (Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Masked Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>), inference alternates between unmasking and (re)masking; these may be interleaved or staged, depending on the method.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Challenge.</span>
The key challenges are twofold: formulating a <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;--ltx-bg-color:#E6F5F3;">principled definition of per-token quality</span>  and <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;--ltx-bg-color:#E6F5F3;">constructing a model</span>  that estimates it. These can be formalized as follows: A per-token quality is a function <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> that takes any partially masked sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and returns position-wise scalars for non-masked positions <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}^{i}\neq\mathbf{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}^{i}\neq\mathbf{m}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\star}\colon(\mathcal{V}\cup\{\mathbf{m}\})^{L}\to[0,1]^{L},\quad{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g^{i}_{\star}({\mathbf{y}})\approx(\text{Per-token quality})}=(\text{``how likely'' ${\mathbf{y}}^{i}$ is given ${\mathbf{y}}$})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">⋆</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">≈</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">Per-token quality</mtext><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>“how likely” </mtext><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mtext> is given </mtext><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\star}\colon(\mathcal{V}\cup\{\mathbf{m}\})^{L}\to[0,1]^{L},\quad{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g^{i}_{\star}({\mathbf{y}})\approx(\text{Per-token quality})}=(\text{``how likely'' ${\mathbf{y}}^{i}$ is given ${\mathbf{y}}$}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The phrase “how likely” is a modeling choice; whatever the choice, it should align with the intended notion of per-token quality (our PRISM instantiation is given in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). The goal is a computationally efficient algorithm for modeling <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> that, in the limit, provably learns the chosen definition of per-token quality. As we argue next, prior work typically lacks either a precise, testable target of per-token quality or entirely changes the MDM design itself, limiting its applicability. We therefore organize the prior work along whether it specifies a precise target for per-token quality on the state during inference <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> and how disruptive it is to the MDM design.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S2.I4" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:10pt;">
<li id="S2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training-free approaches: DFM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, ReMDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, P2-Self <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> are drop-in and can be applied to any pretrained MDM, however they lack a precise target: scores are random (DFM, ReMDM), evaluated on a different input than <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math> (ReMDM-conf), or inferred from logits that were not trained to present the token quality (P2-Self).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Methods that require rehauling MDM design: GIDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">von Rütte et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Informed Corrector <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, indeed articulate a valid target for self-correction but achieve it by changing the pretraining design or architecture, requiring full retraining.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Proxy-<math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> approaches: Token Critic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gou et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and P2-Train <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> train an external scorer to approximate the marginal likelihoods of a sampled clean sequence <math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a given <math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it first samples a reconstructed clean sequence <math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> in a single step, then calculates the quality score via the trained module. Since the pretrained MDM has learned the <em id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">per-token</em> unmasking posterior, not the <em id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">joint posterior</em> across positions, <math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> can deviate a lot from a valid sample from the posterior. This method also requires an extra forward pass for scoring.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a comprehensive explanation of the prior work and their notions of per-token quality in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Related Works ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. <em id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">In short</em>, prior work either lacks a precise target of per-token quality or alters the design of the MDM itself. <em id="S2.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">In contrast</em>, PRISM assigns an explicit per-token target to a given sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math> and estimates it in a single forward pass by fine-tuning the pretrained MDM, while still preserving the MDM’s ability to compute per-token posteriors.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>PRISM</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">P</span>lug-in <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">R</span>emasking for <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">I</span>nference-time <span id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">S</span>elf-correction of <span id="S3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">M</span>asked diffusion models (<span id="S3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">PRISM</span>). We substantiate its theoretical foundation in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Theoretical foundation of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, present our empirical modeling design in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Empirical design choice of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, and explain how PRISM is used during inference in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Employing PRISM at inference ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>. Recall the definition of per-token quality from Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Self-correction for MDM via per-token quality ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\star}\colon(\mathcal{V}\cup\{\mathbf{m}\})^{L}\to[0,1]^{L},\quad{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g^{i}_{\star}({\mathbf{y}})\approx(\text{Per-token quality})}=(\text{``how likely'' ${\mathbf{y}}^{i}$ is given ${\mathbf{y}}$})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">⋆</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">≈</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">Per-token quality</mtext><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>“how likely” </mtext><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mtext> is given </mtext><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\star}\colon(\mathcal{V}\cup\{\mathbf{m}\})^{L}\to[0,1]^{L},\quad{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g^{i}_{\star}({\mathbf{y}})\approx(\text{Per-token quality})}=(\text{``how likely'' ${\mathbf{y}}^{i}$ is given ${\mathbf{y}}$}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A natural candidate for the per-token quality is the ground-truth likelihood of the token <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is masked out of <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote this masked sequence by <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})^{i}=\mathbf{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})^{i}=\mathbf{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})^{j}={\mathbf{y}}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})^{j}={\mathbf{y}}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq i</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our desired notion of <em id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">per-token quality</em> is <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;--ltx-bg-color:#E6F5F3;">the likelihood of a given token conditioned on the rest of the sequence</span>. When <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is consistent with the context <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, this likelihood should be high; otherwise, it should be low, marking the position as a candidate for remasking. Formally,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\star}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}:=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">⋆</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\star}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}:=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">To clarify, <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as the unmasking posterior used for MDM in Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Masked Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discussion on our notion of per-token quality.</span> Since our per-token quality is derived from the posterior, it can be sensitive to uncertainty, e.g., in <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}=([\text{I}],\mathbf{m},[\text{to}],[\text{school}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>I</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>to</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>school</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}=([\text{I}],\mathbf{m},[\text{to}],[\text{school}])</annotation></semantics></math>, although the fourth token <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\text{school}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>school</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\text{school}]</annotation></semantics></math> is valid, <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\star}^{4}({\mathbf{y}})=p({\mathbf{x}}^{4}=[\text{school}]\,|\,([\text{I}],\mathbf{m},[\text{to}],\mathbf{m}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>school</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>I</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>to</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\star}^{4}({\mathbf{y}})=p({\mathbf{x}}^{4}=[\text{school}]\,|\,([\text{I}],\mathbf{m},[\text{to}],\mathbf{m}))</annotation></semantics></math> may be low since there are multiple candidates for that position. In such situations, it cannot “hurt” to remask/resample such tokens, as there is high entropy in the true posterior marginal anyway.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">More importantly, our notion of per-token quality is effective at detecting <em id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">incorrect</em> tokens in positions with low uncertainty, e.g., in <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}=([\text{I}],[\text{eat}],[\text{a}],[\text{apple}],[\text{strudel}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>I</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>eat</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>a</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>apple</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>strudel</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}=([\text{I}],[\text{eat}],[\text{a}],[\text{apple}],[\text{strudel}])</annotation></semantics></math>, the third token <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\text{a}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>a</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\text{a}]</annotation></semantics></math> is ungrammatical given <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\text{apple}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>apple</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\text{apple}]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\star}^{3}({\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\star}^{3}({\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> is sharply concentrated on other words like <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\text{an}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>an</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\text{an}]</annotation></semantics></math>,<math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\text{the}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>the</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\text{the}]</annotation></semantics></math>;
our quality score would correctly identify <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\text{a}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>a</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\text{a}]</annotation></semantics></math> for remasking and revision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Challenge: Unmasking vs. Remasking training.</span> Our goal is to train (or obtain) a model <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> that approximates <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is challenging because <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> must take the sequence where the position <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is already unmasked, yet it must approximate the posterior defined with the <em id="S3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">masked</em> context <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, an MDM <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> predicts from the masked position itself;</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}\approx p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})\in[0,1],\quad{\color[rgb]{0.2539,0.4102,0.8828}f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})}\approx p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})\in\Delta(\mathcal{V})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">f</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#4169E1">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" fence="false" mathcolor="#4169E1" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐳</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}\approx p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})\in[0,1],\quad{\color[rgb]{0.2539,0.4102,0.8828}f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})}\approx p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})\in\Delta(\mathcal{V}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Although <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.2539,0.4102,0.8828}f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">f</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#4169E1">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" fence="false" mathcolor="#4169E1" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">⊕</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐦</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.2539,0.4102,0.8828}f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math>’s <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate target the same quantity, they condition on different inputs. Consequently, <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be simply trained or efficiently queried as in MDM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A naive workaround is a distillation-based approach, supervising <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}</annotation></semantics></math> with the teacher signal <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.2539,0.4102,0.8828}f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}^{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">f</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#4169E1">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" fence="false" mathcolor="#4169E1" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">⊕</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐦</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">i</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.2539,0.4102,0.8828}f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}^{i})}</annotation></semantics></math>. This has two drawbacks: First, even in an infinite data and compute regime, we cannot achieve perfect <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> if the teacher model <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is imperfect. Second, it is data-inefficient: each training pair <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{y}},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{y}},i)</annotation></semantics></math> requires a fresh evaluation of <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, one forward pass of <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> supervises only a single index, i.e., covering <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> clean token positions in a sequence requires <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> passes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To address this input mismatch, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2024)</a></cite> leverage a hollow-transformer <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the property <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{\prime}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})\approx p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{\prime}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})\approx p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling the use of the same loss as MDM. However, this requires adopting a new architecture (with a tweak on the attention mechanism) that processes <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> while equivalently behaving as if position <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> were masked, which entails retraining and limiting plug-and-play applicability to pretrained MDMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">As we will show, PRISM does not possess these limitations: it is plug-and-play with any pretrained MDM <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> (no architectural changes), and—even when <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is imperfect, it recovers the desired per-token quality <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> in the infinite-data limit.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Theoretical foundation of PRISM</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we establish the theoretical foundation of PRISM. We begin by recalling the premise behind the MDM training loss before describing how we adapt this premise to give rise to PRISM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Marginalization perspective on MDM training.</span> We rewrite the MDM training loss in equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Masked Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}}}\left[\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i\colon{\mathbf{z}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}}-\log f_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{x}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}}}\left[\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i\colon{\mathbf{z}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}}-\log f_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{x}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We now aim to understand why the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> has the unmasking posterior as its unique minimizer. First, the expectation over the joint distribution of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math> can be written by first sampling <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> and then <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the induced posterior. For fixed <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the loss only depends on the posterior of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is exactly the <span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;--ltx-bg-color:#ECF0FC;">unmasking posterior</span>  <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we can rewrite:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{z}}\left[\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i\colon{\mathbf{z}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}}{\color[rgb]{0.2539,0.4102,0.8828}\mathbb{E}_{v\sim p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\mid{\mathbf{z}})}\left[-\log f_{\theta}^{i}(v\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})\right]}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">∼</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐱</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">i</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#4169E1" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#4169E1" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐳</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">[</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#4169E1">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">log</mi><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">f</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" fence="false" lspace="0.170em" mathcolor="#4169E1" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐳</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{z}}\left[\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i\colon{\mathbf{z}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}}{\color[rgb]{0.2539,0.4102,0.8828}\mathbb{E}_{v\sim p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\mid{\mathbf{z}})}\left[-\log f_{\theta}^{i}(v\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})\right]}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">and then we observe that the blue-colored term is the cross-entropy between two distributions <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is minimized at <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\theta^{\star}}^{i}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{z}})=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">f</mi><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">θ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">⋆</mo></msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#4169E1" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#4169E1" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐳</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">p</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">i</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#4169E1">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" fence="false" mathcolor="#4169E1" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐳</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{\star}}^{i}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{z}})=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{z}},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{z}},i)</annotation></semantics></math>. Put differently, the masking process of MDM defines the joint distribution over <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math> and fixing <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <em id="S3.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">marginalizing</em> over <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> yields a cross-entropy between the network output and the desired unmasking posterior. We use this marginalization technique to build PRISM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PRISM.</span> Recall our goal for training <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> to predict <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;--ltx-bg-color:#E6F5F3;">per-token quality</span>, formally defined as <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi^{\star}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">⋆</mo></msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">=</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#009F86" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">⊕</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐦</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi^{\star}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a pretrained MDM <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, PRISM proceeds as follows.</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:17pt;">
<li id="S3.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Construct a pair <math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math> as in MDM training: sample <math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> and then draw <math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> by masking.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sample <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>: choose a masked index <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> and replace it with a clean token <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}^{i}\sim f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}^{i}\sim f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Step (a) defines the same joint distribution over <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math> as MDM. Step (b) yields a random variable <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{y}},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{y}},i)</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Although we do not designate a sampling rule for the index <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, PRISM’s guarantee is invariant to its rule, e.g., uniformly random, confidence-based. As a result, PRISM induces a joint distribution over <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i))</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking expectation over this joint distribution, we define the PRISM loss:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\phi)\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}\,)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>BCE</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\phi)\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}\,)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{BCE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>BCE</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{BCE}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a Binary Cross-Entropy loss <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{BCE}(b,p)\!=\!-b\log p\!-\!(1\!-\!b)\log(1\!-\!p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>BCE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{BCE}(b,p)\!=\!-b\log p\!-\!(1\!-\!b)\log(1\!-\!p)</annotation></semantics></math> for a binary label <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\!\in\!\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\!\in\!\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This loss is the crux of PRISM, whose unique minimizer is the true per-token quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS1.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="58.89" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 58.89" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,58.89) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 6.23 L 0 52.66 C 0 56.1 2.79 58.89 6.23 58.89 L 543.77 58.89 C 547.21 58.89 550 56.1 550 52.66 L 550 6.23 C 550 2.79 547.21 0 543.77 0 L 6.23 0 C 2.79 0 0 2.79 0 6.23 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 6.23 L 0.69 52.66 C 0.69 55.72 3.17 58.2 6.23 58.2 L 543.77 58.2 C 546.83 58.2 549.31 55.72 549.31 52.66 L 549.31 6.23 C 549.31 3.17 546.83 0.69 543.77 0.69 L 6.23 0.69 C 3.17 0.69 0.69 3.17 0.69 6.23 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.99 43.95)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.45em;--ltx-fo-height:0.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:2.73em;font-size:10pt;" height="47.41" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.69)" width="532.04"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.45em;">
<span id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(PRISM)<span id="Thmproposition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<span id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The per-token quality uniquely minimizes the PRISM loss <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ϕ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> equation <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.1 Theoretical foundation of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>:</span></span>
<span id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<span><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{\star}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ϕ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⋆</mo></msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐲</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊕</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐦</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{\star}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Before proving Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (PRISM). ‣ 3.1 Theoretical foundation of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we highlight two key advantages that PRISM offers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Advantages.</span> First, the minimizer <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{\star}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{\star}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S3.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">does not</em> depend on the specific choice of <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> affects the training objective only through the marginalized distribution of <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{y}},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{y}},i)</annotation></semantics></math>, not the minimizer. This contrasts with the aforementioned distillation-based approach, in which an imperfect <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> will not give rise to a perfect <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Second, PRISM naturally adapts to a pretrained MDM; a single model can share the same architecture and carry two heads, one for the unmasking posterior and one for per-token quality. We detail the modeling choices of PRISM in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Empirical design choice of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PRISM: Guarantee.</span> Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (PRISM). ‣ 3.1 Theoretical foundation of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can be demonstrated within a few lines. The key mechanism is that the joint distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i))</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits a simple posterior given <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{y}},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{y}},i)</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is deterministically <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}={\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}={\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and each <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from the same unmasking posterior of MDM, i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="v\sim p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\sim p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, fixing <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{y}},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{y}},i)</annotation></semantics></math> and then <em id="S3.SS1.p8.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">marginalizing</em> the binary label <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math> yields the simple byproduct,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q}\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{v\sim p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})}\left[\mathbf{1}[v={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\right]=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">q</mi><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q}\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{v\sim p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})}\left[\mathbf{1}[v={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\right]=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">the per-token quality. We then expand the binary cross-entropy loss:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\phi)=\mathbb{E}_{({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[-{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q}\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})-({\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}1-q})\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}1-g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">q</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\phi)=\mathbb{E}_{({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[-{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q}\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})-({\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}1-q})\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}1-g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.3" class="ltx_p">which is exactly minimized at <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi^{\star}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">⋆</mo></msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">=</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#009F86" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">⊕</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐦</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi^{\star}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Empirical design choice of PRISM</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present our empirical modeling choices that go into the design of PRISM. We first explain the plug-and-play aspect of PRISM, in which we adapt a pretrained MDM and fine-tune it via the PRISM loss.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01384v1/PRISM_pipeline_TK.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/119;" width="548" height="119"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PRISM training pipeline.</span> Fine-tuning samples are built in two steps; <span id="S3.F2.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span> masking process to obtain <math id="S3.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S3.F2.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span> unmask a few indices in <math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> to get <math id="S3.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a pretrained MDM <math id="S3.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">f</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S3.F2.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left</span>). We add a <em id="S3.F2.15" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;">lightweight adapter</em> to a pretrained MDM so that the resulting model computes the <em id="S3.F2.16" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">unmasking posterior (<math id="S3.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">f</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>)</em> and <em id="S3.F2.17" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;">per-token quality</em><span id="S3.F2.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;"> (<math id="S3.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> (<span id="S3.F2.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right</span>).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Plug-and-Play approach.</span>
Assume a pretrained MDM <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is given, which by design has learned the unmasking posterior. PRISM augments this model with a lightweight auxiliary adapter that reuses the same backbone to produce a size-<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> tensor. In words, <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;--ltx-bg-color:#EFECF5;">the unmasking posterior and per-token quality share a single backbone and are computed by separate heads</span>  (Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Empirical design choice of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For a given sequence, we respectively denote the two head outputs as <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the final hidden state of the MDM backbone. The unmasking posterior <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by passing <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> through the unmasking head (which originally exists in pretrained MDM) and then applying the softmax. The per-token quality <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by passing <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> through the attached head and applying the sigmoid. Sigmoid is used to guarantee <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\theta}^{i}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\theta}^{i}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, making it suitable for the binary cross-entropy loss. Thus, with a single <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and a lightweight adapter, we <em id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">jointly model</em> the unmasking posterior and then the per-token quality: for a partially masked sequence <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{w}})}\approx p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{w}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{w}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})\in[0,1],\quad{\color[rgb]{0.2539,0.4102,0.8828}f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{w}})}\approx p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{w}})\in\Delta(\mathcal{V})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐰</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">f</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#4169E1">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" fence="false" mathcolor="#4169E1" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐰</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{w}})}\approx p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{w}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{w}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})\in[0,1],\quad{\color[rgb]{0.2539,0.4102,0.8828}f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{w}})}\approx p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{w}})\in\Delta(\mathcal{V}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">At a high level, the PRISM recipe is simple: train <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> using the PRISM loss defined in equation <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.1 Theoretical foundation of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Consequently, the parameters updated through <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> are those of the attached head and the backbone, excluding the unmasking head. As an alternative to fine-tuning the backbone, one can add a LoRA adapter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> into the backbone and train only the LoRA adapter parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Practical interventions.</span> We introduce two practical interventions that improve PRISM’s efficiency. First, to preserve <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>’s ability to estimate the unmasking posterior while <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is learning toward the per-token quality, we add the MDM training loss equation <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.1 Theoretical foundation of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> as a regularization term. For a regularization constant <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the total loss is therefore:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i)}\Bigg[{\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}\,)}+\lambda\times\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j\colon{\mathbf{z}}^{j}=\mathbf{m}}{\color[rgb]{0.2539,0.4102,0.8828}-\log f_{\theta}^{j}({\mathbf{x}}^{j}\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})}\Bigg]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mi>BCE</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#4169E1">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">log</mi><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">f</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">j</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">j</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" fence="false" mathcolor="#4169E1" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐳</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i)}\Bigg[{\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}\,)}+\lambda\times\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j\colon{\mathbf{z}}^{j}=\mathbf{m}}{\color[rgb]{0.2539,0.4102,0.8828}-\log f_{\theta}^{j}({\mathbf{x}}^{j}\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})}\Bigg].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Second, in the sampling process for constructing PRISM training pairs <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i))</annotation></semantics></math>, we modify step (b). To obtain <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than unmasking a single <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain multiple <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{y}},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{y}},i)</annotation></semantics></math> pairs from one <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This produces multiple training pairs <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i))</annotation></semantics></math> with varying <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{y}},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{y}},i)</annotation></semantics></math> values from a single forward pass of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\theta}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math>, improving data efficiency. Since this procedure of sample construction minimizes <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> (not <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>), we apply stop-gradient to this <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. The pseudocode is given as Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2 Empirical design choice of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Importantly, the theoretical guarantee in Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (PRISM). ‣ 3.1 Theoretical foundation of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> still maintains: the true minimizer <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> of this modified PRISM loss continues to encode meaningful per-token quality. We defer the precise statement and proof to Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 PRISM provably learns the per-token quality ‣ Appendix B Training algorithms and guarantees of PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>. Moreover, we conduct a careful ablation of different design choices on sampling <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3 Ablation on Fine-tuning Hyperparameters-(𝑘,𝑛_𝑦) ‣ Appendix D Experiment details and ablation studies ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A4.SS4" title="D.4 Ablation on Fine-tuning Hyperparameters-Nucleus sampling ‣ Appendix D Experiment details and ablation studies ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Practical advantages</span>. PRISM formulation not only guarantees to minimize the ground truth per-token quality but also has two practical advantages. First, since <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is already pretrained, its hidden states encode useful representations, likely accelerating <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>’s training on the per-token quality. Second, the targeted per-token quality is a scalar per position, in contrast to the per-position distribution by the unmasking posterior, making it substantially easier to learn. Perhaps surprisingly, we observe that using much less compute compared to MDM pretraining already yields a meaningful learning of the per-token quality (which we detail these results in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), reinforcing the perspective that PRISM is a cheap <em id="S3.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fine-tuning</em> intervention.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> PRISM fine-tuning from a pretrained MDM</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Require:</span> Pretrained MDM backbone <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">f</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, per-token quality head <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, optimizer <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>, true distribution <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, regularization constant <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, number of unmasking tokens <math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
            Sample <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{data}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{data}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the masking process.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
              
            Initialize the loss <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ \mathcal{L}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ \mathcal{L}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> converges

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;"># Sample<span id="alg1.l5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> </span>from<span id="alg1.l5.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> </span>with<span id="alg1.l5.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> <math id="alg1.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathrm{sg}(\theta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">f</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">θ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathrm{sg}(\theta)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
              
              
              Select a subset <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\{i\mid{\mathbf{z}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\{i\mid{\mathbf{z}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|=k</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
              
              
              Obtain <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l7.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="v^{i}\sim{\color[rgb]{1,0,0}f_{\mathrm{sg}(\theta)}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">f</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">θ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#FF0000">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#FF0000" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">𝐳</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{i}\sim{\color[rgb]{1,0,0}f_{\mathrm{sg}(\theta)}^{i}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="alg1.l7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;"># Calculate Loss</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)\leftarrow\mathcal{L}(\theta)+\frac{1}{|\mathcal{S}|}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{S}}{\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}}\,)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></msub><mi>BCE</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)\leftarrow\mathcal{L}(\theta)+\frac{1}{|\mathcal{S}|}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{S}}{\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}}\,)}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l9.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l9.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">PRISM Loss</span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)\leftarrow\mathcal{L}(\theta)+\lambda\times\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j:{\mathbf{z}}^{j}=\mathbf{m}}{\color[rgb]{0.2539,0.4102,0.8828}-\log f_{\theta}^{j}({\mathbf{x}}^{j}\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#4169E1">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">log</mi><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">f</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">j</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">j</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" fence="false" mathcolor="#4169E1" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1">𝐳</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)\leftarrow\mathcal{L}(\theta)+\lambda\times\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j:{\mathbf{z}}^{j}=\mathbf{m}}{\color[rgb]{0.2539,0.4102,0.8828}-\log f_{\theta}^{j}({\mathbf{x}}^{j}\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="alg1.l10.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l10.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;"> Regularization (MDM loss)</span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\leftarrow\mathcal{O}(\theta,\mathcal{L}(\theta))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\leftarrow\mathcal{O}(\theta,\mathcal{L}(\theta))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">12:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Employing PRISM at inference</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we explain how a model resulting from PRISM is used to perform remasking at inference. The high-level procedure follows Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Masked Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>; at each inference step, we perform remasking and unmasking simultaneously, using the learned unmasking posterior and per-token quality, respectively. Assume we are given a fine-tuned MDM <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> (via PRISM loss) and a monotonically decreasing grid of times <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}=1&gt;\dots&gt;t_{N}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}=1&gt;\dots&gt;t_{N}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for inference. We initialize <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{0}}=(\mathbf{m},\dots,\mathbf{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{0}}=(\mathbf{m},\dots,\mathbf{m})</annotation></semantics></math>. At each step <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell+1}}</annotation></semantics></math> by these following steps.</p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:17pt;">
<li id="S3.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Choose a subset of masked tokens <math id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> to <span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;--ltx-bg-color:#ECF0FC;">unmask</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Choose a subset of clean tokens <math id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to <span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;--ltx-bg-color:#E6F5F3;">remask</span>  with the lowest quality scores <math id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\theta}({\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\theta}({\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;--ltx-bg-color:#E6F5F3;">remask</span>  <math id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and for each <math id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;--ltx-bg-color:#ECF0FC;">unmask</span>  <math id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> to a clean token sampled from <math id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\theta}^{j}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}})\in\Delta(\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}^{j}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}})\in\Delta(\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We emphasize that <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\theta}^{\cdot}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>⋅</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}^{\cdot}(\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\theta}({\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\theta}({\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}})</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained from a <em id="S3.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single</em> forward pass of the backbone. Consequently, <span id="S3.SS3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;--ltx-bg-color:#EFECF5;">our method does not add any computational overhead</span>  relative to methods without remasking (vanilla MDM inference) or training-free approaches, e.g, ReMDM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Design choices.</span> There are three design choices in this inference procedure: the number of tokens to unmask at each step–<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|</annotation></semantics></math>, the rule for choosing <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the number of tokens to remask at each step–<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{T}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{T}|</annotation></semantics></math>. As our focus is on PRISM’s remasking strategy, we do not modify the design choices regarding the rule for selecting <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> but follow prior seminal approaches. We also keep the rule for selecting <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{T}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{T}|</annotation></semantics></math> as simple as possible; For unmasking schemes that assign <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|</annotation></semantics></math> stochastically, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al. (2024)</a></cite>), <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|\sim\mathrm{Binom}(n,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Binom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|\sim\mathrm{Binom}(n,p)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the discretized time steps and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of masked tokens in a given sequence. Here we draw <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{T}|\sim\mathrm{Binom}(L-n,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Binom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{T}|\sim\mathrm{Binom}(L-n,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and accordingly set <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|\sim|\mathcal{T}|+\mathrm{Binom}(n,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>Binom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|\sim|\mathcal{T}|+\mathrm{Binom}(n,p)</annotation></semantics></math> to preserve the total unmasked count’s expectation. For schemes that preallocate <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|</annotation></semantics></math>s uniformly before inference, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al. (2025)</a></cite>), we likewise preallocate <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{T}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{T}|</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly across the time steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we demonstrate that PRISM is a practically efficient and scalable approach across different domains and scales. In Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Motivating example: Sudoku puzzle ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we apply PRISM to a <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math>M-scale MDM for Sudoku. In Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Unconditional Text Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we apply PRISM to a 170M-scale MDM and evaluate its ability for natural language modeling. Finally, in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Appyling PRISM to 8B MDM ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, we apply PRISM to LLaDA-8B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and evaluate it on a coding task. We first outline the pipeline shared across all experiments. We provide the experimental details in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Experiment details and ablation studies ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experiment pipeline.</span>
We start from an MDM pretrained for each domain and attach a lightweight adapter. We then fine-tune the model on the PRISM loss defined in equation <a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.2 Empirical design choice of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. For baselines, we adopt ReMDM (ReMDM-cap or ReMDM-loop) and ReMDM-conf from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite>; for fair comparison, we re-evaluate these baselines using the same backbone after PRISM fine-tuning. Finally, since our focus is on the remasking strategy, we restrict comparisons to remasking baselines. We summarize our experimental results as follows:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:10pt;">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#8B4512;--ltx-bg-color:#F3ECE7;">Efficiency</span>: The PRISM fine-tuned MDM has the ability to self-correct using much fewer samples compared to those used for pretraining.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#8B4512;--ltx-bg-color:#F3ECE7;">Performance</span>: The PRISM-tuned MDMs outperform ReMDM and ReMDM-conf, indicating that PRISM learns our desired notion of per-token quality quite well.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Motivating example: Sudoku puzzle</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we examine PRISM on Sudoku puzzles and show that the learned per-token quality operates as desired.
Before moving on to the results, we use Sudoku as a <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">metaphorical testbed</em> to explain why ReMDM and ReMDM-conf can fail to capture the intended per-token quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sudoku as a metaphorical testbed.</span>
ReMDM assigns token quality randomly, which can unnecessarily flag correct cells. ReMDM-conf sets the token quality to the likelihood at the time a cell was unmasked; this is computed on a previous Sudoku board state and can quickly become misaligned from the current board. In contrast, PRISM predicts per-token quality under the current board, thereby reliably identifying incorrect cells.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:182.8pt;">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> We construct a corpus of Sudoku puzzles (<math id="S4.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>48</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48</annotation></semantics></math>k for training and <math id="S4.SS1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>k for evaluation), following the setup of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ben-Hamu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alp, 2024</a>)</cite>. Each board is tokenized as a length-<math id="S4.SS1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="89" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>89</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">89</annotation></semantics></math> sequence comprising <math id="S4.SS1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="81" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>81</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">81</annotation></semantics></math> cell values and <math id="S4.SS1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> [<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EOL</span>] tokens that separate columns. Following the MDM pretraining recipe of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al. (2024)</a></cite>, we use a <math id="S4.SS1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>28.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28.6</annotation></semantics></math>M Diffusion Transformer (DiT; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peebles &amp; Xie (2023)</a></cite>) architecture as the backbone and pretrain it for <math id="S4.SS1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>k iterations (<math id="S4.SS1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 530" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>530</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 530</annotation></semantics></math> epochs). We then fine-tune it with the PRISM loss, additionally with a lightweight adapter (<math id="S4.SS1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.13</annotation></semantics></math>M parameters).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:206.7pt;">
        
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para"><img src="2510.01384v1/merged_sudoku_hq.png" id="S4.SS1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:285/141;" width="285" height="141" alt="[Uncaptioned image]">
</div>
        
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_center">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S4.F3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> PRISM achieves higher success accuracy on Sudoku puzzles than baselines. (<span id="S4.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_underline" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red line</span>) <span id="S4.F3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> PRISM detects the incorrect cells by assigning low per-token quality (<span id="S4.F3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_underline" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red-colored cells</span>).</figcaption>
</figure>
      
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> We evaluate the success rate for the same model across epochs for PRISM and baselines. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 Motivating example: Sudoku puzzle ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, PRISM achieves progressively higher success rates during fine-tuning (<span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red line</span>) and surpasses the baselines. This confirms that the learned per-token quality enables effective self-correction at inference. Notably, PRISM starts to outperform vanilla MDM inference (<span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_ulem_dashuline" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">dashed line</span>) and baselines within only a few epochs, far fewer than the pretraining epochs (<math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 530" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>530</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 530</annotation></semantics></math>), corroborating our claim of sample efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visualization of learned per-token quality.</span> Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 Motivating example: Sudoku puzzle ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> further visualizes PRISM’s ability to detect incorrect tokens. On the shown board, three digits are misfilled: <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,6)=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,6)=8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(5,5)=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(5,5)=4</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7,3)=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7,3)=7</annotation></semantics></math>. The computed per-token quality assigns low scores to these cells (<span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red</span>), while other cells attain high scores, nearly <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.99</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.99</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">blue</span>), indicating that the fine-tuned model has almost perfectly learned the per-token quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Unconditional Text Generation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup</span>. We first take a DiT-based MDM with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="170" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>170</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">170</annotation></semantics></math>M parameters, using a checkpoint from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. This MDM is pretrained on the OpenWebText corpus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gokaslan &amp; Cohen, 2019</a>)</cite> with the GPT-2 tokenizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., </a>)</cite>, a maximum sequence length <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=1024</annotation></semantics></math>, and a total of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="262" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>262</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">262</annotation></semantics></math>B tokens. We then attach a lightweight <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>M parameter adapter, one attention layer applied to the final hidden state, and fine-tune it with the PRISM loss on a total <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="164" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>164</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">164</annotation></semantics></math>M tokens from the same OpenWebText corpus. This fine-tuning is data-efficient, using <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{1600}\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏𝟔𝟎𝟎</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1600}\times</annotation></semantics></math> fewer tokens than pretraining.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> We then assess the performance of PRISM and baselines on unconditional generation. Our primary metrics are Generative Perplexity and MAUVE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pillutla et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, which respectively assess per-sequence fluency and distributional similarity between generated samples and the reference distribution. Moreover, given the prior observation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite> that a sequence with too many redundant tokens can spuriously yield better (i.e., lower) generative perplexity, we also report the entropy as a sanity metric. Following standard evaluation protocols <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pillutla et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we generate <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5000</annotation></semantics></math> samples to compute both metrics, using GPT-2-Large as the reference model. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Unconditional Text Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, PRISM
outperforms baselines on both metrics (<span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red lines</span>) while achieving a comparable entropy, especially in the regime with fewer sampling steps (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N&lt;1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N&lt;1024</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sampling strategy.</span>
As sentence diversity is crucial for unconditional generation, we observe that the vanilla PRISM inference from Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Employing PRISM at inference ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> can degrade diversity. We attribute this to the greedy nature of the token-remasking policy that ranks positions by per-token quality. To mitigate this, we adopt <em id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">PRISM-loop</em>, analogous to ReMDM-loop <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: follow PRISM’s remasking scheme and add <em id="S4.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">loop</em> steps at <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{N}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{N}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. After obtaining a clean sequence, in a loop, we randomly remask a subset of tokens and unmask them. A full breakdown of the sampling strategy is given in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 PRISM-Loop ‣ Appendix C Employing PRISM at inference ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>,  <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Details on the remasking Strategy for OpenwebText ‣ Appendix D Experiment details and ablation studies ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01384v1/gen_quality_owt_plot.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/180;" width="548" height="180"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S4.F4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assessing PRISM on unconditional text generation</span>. PRISM (<span id="S4.F4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_underline" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red</span>) exhibits superior performance compared to baselines (ReMDM: <span id="S4.F4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_underline" style="--ltx-fg-color:#036C3A;">green</span>, ReMDM-conf: <span id="S4.F4.10" class="ltx_text ltx_underline" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;">blue</span>, Vanilla MDM: <span id="S4.F4.11" class="ltx_text ltx_underline" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">gray</span>), particularly in the regime with fewer sampling steps (<math id="S4.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N&lt;1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N&lt;1024</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Appyling PRISM to <math id="S4.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>B MDM</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> We take LLaDA-<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, an open-sourced <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>B MDM. While freezing the backbone, we add an additional head and a LoRA adapter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, for a total of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 250" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>250</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 250</annotation></semantics></math>M trainable parameters. We fine-tune it using the PRISM loss on the opc-sft-stage2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math>M pairs) for <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> epochs. Fine-tuning completes in <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 30</annotation></semantics></math> hours on <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> H100 GPUs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:198.7pt;">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> We evaluate the resulting model in a zero-shot setting on MBPP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021b</a>)</cite>, a challenging Python coding benchmark with <math id="S4.SS3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="974" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>974</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">974</annotation></semantics></math> problems. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.3 Appyling PRISM to 8B MDM ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, PRISM outperforms ReMDM and ReMDM-conf, especially in the regime of <math id="S4.SS3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math> sampling steps, by approximately <math id="S4.SS3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.7\%</annotation></semantics></math>. We detail training and inference configurations in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS5" title="D.5 Experiment Configurations ‣ Appendix D Experiment details and ablation studies ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.5</span></a>, <a href="#A4.SS6" title="D.6 Additional experimental details on LLaDA ‣ Appendix D Experiment details and ablation studies ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:190.8pt;">
<table id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sampling steps</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">256</th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">512</th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">1024</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">PRISM</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">23.0%</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">30.5%</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">32.9%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">ReMDM</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">20.2%</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">29.7%</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T1.2.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">33.2</span>%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">ReMDM-conf</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">20.3%</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">30.0%</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">32.7%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">MDM</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">18.6%</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">28.2%</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">32.7%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PRISM on LLaDA-8B-Instruct</span>. </figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discussion on experimental results.</span>
MDMs model per-position unmasking posteriors rather than the joint distribution over positions. Consequently, with few-step inference, they are prone to dependency errors across positions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which creates a natural opportunity for our self-correction mechanism to detect erroneous predictions. We confirm this empirically: methods that incorporate remasking outperform vanilla MDM inference as the number of sampling steps decreases (Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Unconditional Text Generation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.3 Appyling PRISM to 8B MDM ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). In particular, PRISM achieves larger gains than baselines by learning per-token quality. However, as the number of sampling steps increases, partially unmasked sequences contain fewer errors, leaving less headroom for the self-correction. For the scaling-up experiments in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Appyling PRISM to 8B MDM ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, although we fine-tune LLaDA-8B-Instruct on only 0.1M dataset pairs and evaluate it on MBPP, we expect that a more generalized model could be obtained by constructing a richer and more diverse dataset, given sufficient compute.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Outlook.</span> We introduced PRISM, a plug-and-play fine-tuning framework that equips pretrained MDMs with self-correction ability, addressing prevalent limitations of prior work. PRISM operates under a theoretically grounded fine-tuning loss and shows efficiency across multiple domains and scales.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A unique feature of the PRISM loss is that it provably learns per-token quality without fully generating a clean sequence. This sheds light on PRISM’s potential compared to other approaches, such as reinforcement learning, where full sequence generation is necessary to obtain a learning signal. That said, our notion of per-token quality score has limits; as it is based on the per-position posterior marginals, it cannot fully capture global errors, e.g., reasoning. An important direction for future work is to develop frameworks that enable discrete diffusion models to detect global reasoning errors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Stepping back, this work is part of a broader program to build generative models that compose discrete sequences the way that humans do–through correction and reordering. The flexibility of MDMs at inference time offers a key stepping stone towards this goal.</p>
</div>
</section>
