<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Masked Diffusion Models.</span>
Masked Diffusion Models (MDMs) were previously built on continuous-time Markov chains over discrete spaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoogeboom et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Subsequently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al. (2021a)</a></cite> introduced D3PM with several families of transition kernels, followed by SEDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. These discrete diffusions can also be understood through the lens of discrete flows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which parallel flow matching and stochastic interpolants over continuous spaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Albergo &amp; Vanden-Eijnden, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Albergo et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Among transition-kernel designs, the absorbing state (<em id="A1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">masking</em>) kernel has been particularly popular due to its simple formulation and training objective. Subsequent work established a theoretical framework for these models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Follow-up works have scaled MDMs up to billions of parameters, showcasing their potential compared to autoregressive models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepMind, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labs et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference-time strategies for discrete diffusion models.</span>
A key feature of MDMs is their inference-time flexibility, highlighted by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al. (2024)</a></cite> and aligned with our any-order perspective in Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Masked Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>. Recall the MDM inference procedure from Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Masked Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>:</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:15pt;">
<li id="A1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Choose a subset of masked positions <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\{i\mid{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\{i\mid{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> to <span id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;--ltx-bg-color:#ECF0FC;">unmask</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;--ltx-bg-color:#ECF0FC;">unmask</span>  by sampling a clean token from <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}})\in\Delta(\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}})\in\Delta(\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math> and setting <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> accordingly.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The choice of <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is entirely flexible, and an appropriate selection can substantially improve downstream task performance. Since our work focuses on self-correction, i.e., re-masking, we only briefly survey prior work on unmasking strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ben-Hamu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rout et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. A complementary line of work studies inference-time planning strategies for discrete diffusion with uniform transition kernels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Varma et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; these methods fall outside the scope of MDMs.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Details on prior work on remasking strategy (self-correction) for MDM</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we detail our discussion in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Self-correction for MDM via per-token quality ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, focusing on their different notions of per-token qualities. We recall the notion of per-token quality stated in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Self-correction for MDM via per-token quality ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</p>
<table id="A1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\star}\colon(\mathcal{V}\cup\{\mathbf{m}\})^{L}\to[0,1]^{L},\quad{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g^{i}_{\star}({\mathbf{y}})\approx(\text{Per-token quality})}=(\text{``how likely'' ${\mathbf{y}}^{i}$ is given ${\mathbf{y}}$})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">⋆</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">≈</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">Per-token quality</mtext><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>“how likely” </mtext><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mtext> is given </mtext><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\star}\colon(\mathcal{V}\cup\{\mathbf{m}\})^{L}\to[0,1]^{L},\quad{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g^{i}_{\star}({\mathbf{y}})\approx(\text{Per-token quality})}=(\text{``how likely'' ${\mathbf{y}}^{i}$ is given ${\mathbf{y}}$}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<ul id="A1.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DFM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gat et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and <span id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ReMDM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> randomly remask clean tokens in <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is equivalent to assigning i.i.d. per-token qualities <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\star}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})\sim\operatorname{Unif}[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\star}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})\sim\operatorname{Unif}[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P2-Self</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> defines per-token quality using the pretrained MDM’s score for the observed token under input <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g., the probability <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{y}}^{i}\mid{\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{y}}^{i}\mid{\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Given that <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}^{i}\neq\mathbf{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}^{i}\neq\mathbf{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model was never trained on this index in this state; it only encodes a meaningful quantity (unmasking posterior) when <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}^{i}=\mathbf{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. This notion of per-token quality is therefore not grounded.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ReMDM-conf</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> takes the per-token quality to be the likelihood at the step the token was unmasked: if position <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> was unmasked at <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}&gt;t_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}&gt;t_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., an earlier step), set <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\star}^{i}:=f_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{x}}_{t_{k}}^{i}\mid{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{k}})=f_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}\mid{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\star}^{i}:=f_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{x}}_{t_{k}}^{i}\mid{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{k}})=f_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}^{i}\mid{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math>. As noted, this ignores the current state <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math> and can therefore be misaligned with the model’s present beliefs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Token Critic</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lezama et al., 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lezama et al., 2022b</a>)</cite> and <span id="A1.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P2-train</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> train an auxiliary scorer for the likelihood of a given prediction of clean sequence <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math>, they first form a full completion <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> by unmasking each mask token at position <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,\mid\,{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}^{i}(\cdot\,\mid\,{\mathbf{x}}_{t_{\ell}})</annotation></semantics></math>, then evaluate quality by passing this <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the external module. A key limitation is that <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from per-token unmasking posteriors, not the ground-truth posterior across positions; thus, even with a perfect pretrained MDM, <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{s}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{s}}}</annotation></semantics></math> can deviate substantially from the true likelihood <math id="A1.I2.i4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, losing a theoretical guarantee.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GIDD</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">von Rütte et al., 2025</a>)</cite> introduces a family of transition kernels that linearly combine uniform and absorbing (masking) kernels. Models pretrained under this kernel encode posteriors at clean-token positions–however, this requires training under the new kernel and is not directly applicable to an off-the-shelf pretrained MDM, altogether losing the benefit of MDM over other types of kernels.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Informed corrector</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> employs a hollow-transformer <math id="A1.I2.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="A1.I2.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{\prime}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})=g_{\phi^{\prime}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})\approx p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\mid{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{\prime}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})=g_{\phi^{\prime}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})\approx p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\mid{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling the same training loss as MDM. This, however, mandates a modified architecture (attention tweak) that processes <math id="A1.I2.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> as if position <math id="A1.I2.i6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> were masked, necessitating retraining.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In summary, while these methods improve over vanilla MDM inference without remasking, they either substantially reshape the MDM pipeline or rely on imprecise notions and proxies for per-token quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Training algorithms and guarantees of PRISM</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Marginal Equivalence of the Masking Process</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize that the two masking procedures stated in Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Masked Diffusion Models ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> induce the same conditional distribution of <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the same joint distributions <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}})</annotation></semantics></math>. It suffices to show they generate the same distribution over masked index sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consider (b): draw <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim\mathrm{Unif}[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim\mathrm{Unif}[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and for each <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, replace <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}^{i}\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}^{i}\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}</annotation></semantics></math> with probability <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The probability of <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> tokens to be masked is:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{0}^{1}\binom{L}{n}t^{n}(1-t)^{L-n}dt=\binom{L}{n}\mathrm{B}(n+1,\,L-n+1)=\frac{1}{L+1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{0}^{1}\binom{L}{n}t^{n}(1-t)^{L-n}dt=\binom{L}{n}\mathrm{B}(n+1,\,L-n+1)=\frac{1}{L+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{B}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes Beta function. This coincides with procedure (a), where <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly sampled from <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1,\dots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1,\dots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>PRISM provably learns the per-token quality</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we restate Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (PRISM). ‣ 3.1 Theoretical foundation of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and detail its proof. The proof essentially follows Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Theoretical foundation of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, but we provide the detailed calculations here.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (PRISM). ‣ 3.1 Theoretical foundation of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (restated).</span>
Let the PRISM loss be</p>
<table id="A2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\phi)=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i)}\!\left[\mathrm{BCE}\big(\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}],\,g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})\big)\right],\quad\mathrm{BCE}(b,p)=-\,b\log p-(1-b)\log(1-p)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>BCE</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>BCE</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.392em">−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\phi)=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i)}\!\left[\mathrm{BCE}\big(\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}],\,g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})\big)\right],\quad\mathrm{BCE}(b,p)=-\,b\log p-(1-b)\log(1-p).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Then the unique minimizer satisfies, for every <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{\star}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})\;=\;p\!\left({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,\middle|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.333em;" width="0.333em"><mi>p</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{\star}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})\;=\;p\!\left({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,\middle|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, the expectation over the joint distribution <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i))</annotation></semantics></math> can be written by first sampling <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{y}},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{y}},i)</annotation></semantics></math> and then <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{z}},{\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{z}},{\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> from the induced posterior. We observe that this induced posterior admits a handy expression:</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}={\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i},\quad v\sim p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}={\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i},\quad v\sim p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Here <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the unmasking posterior given by the MDM’s masking process (Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Theoretical foundation of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), and to avoid notational confusion, we introduce a dummy variable <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, we expand the expectation term.</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\phi)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\phi)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex15.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[\mathbb{E}_{v\sim p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}}),z={\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}}\left[\mathrm{BCE}\big(\mathbf{1}[v={\mathbf{y}}^{i}],\,{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}\big)\right]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>BCE</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[\mathbb{E}_{v\sim p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}}),z={\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}}\left[\mathrm{BCE}\big(\mathbf{1}[v={\mathbf{y}}^{i}],\,{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}\big)\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[-{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q}\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})-({\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}1-q})\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}1-g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">q</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[-{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q}\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})-({\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}1-q})\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}1-g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">where</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q}\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{v\sim p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})}\left[\mathbf{1}[v={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\right]=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">q</mi><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q}\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{v\sim p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})}\left[\mathbf{1}[v={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\right]=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we conclude that <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{\star}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})=q=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{\star}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})=q=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\,|\,{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{q}(x)=-q\log x-(1-q)\log(1-x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{q}(x)=-q\log x-(1-q)\log(1-x)</annotation></semantics></math> is minimized at <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=q\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=q\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Extension on PRISM’s provable guarantee</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide the pseudocode of the practically used PRISM fine-tuning pipeline and then state its theoretical guarantee.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We note that the choice of index set <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> at line 5 of Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2 Empirical design choice of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is flexible; it can either be random or confidence-based. To clarify, at line 6, although we use <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the stop-gradient operation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the process of obtaining <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> can be interpreted as a process of obtaining multiple <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{y}},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{y}},i)</annotation></semantics></math> pairs for each <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under this, the expectation over <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},\mathcal{S}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},\mathcal{S}))</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the expectation over <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i))</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, both formulations of the loss function are valid and equivalent.</p>
<table id="A2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},\mathcal{S})}\left[\frac{1}{|\mathcal{S}|}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{S}}{\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}}\,)}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[{\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}}\,)}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></munder><mi>BCE</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>BCE</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},\mathcal{S})}\left[\frac{1}{|\mathcal{S}|}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{S}}{\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}}\,)}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[{\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}}\,)}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Now we provide the theoretical guarantee on our PRISM loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Provable guarantee of PRISM-extension)<span id="Thmproposition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a fixed <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq L</annotation></semantics></math>, the PRISM loss</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\phi)=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[{\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}}\,)}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>BCE</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\phi)=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[{\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}}\,)}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">has the unique minimizer</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi^{\star}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{S}\sim\Pi(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\mid{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\mathcal{S}})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">⋆</mo></msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi^{\star}}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{S}\sim\Pi(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\mid{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\mathcal{S}})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the expectation is taken over the posterior distribution over <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, induced by the joint distribution above. Here <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence obtained from <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing its <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th token with <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, aligning the notation in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Theoretical foundation of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Before proving this, we clarify some notations. The notation <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> extends the notation <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Theoretical foundation of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> into the case where we mask multiple tokens, more precisely, <math id="A2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\{i\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\{i\}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Also, note that the posterior distribution <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{S}\sim\Pi(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\sim\Pi(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}},i)</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally induced from the defined joint distribution. To clarify, this posterior inherits a dependence on the rule that we choose the index set <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> during the fine-tuning (and also <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>). This also satisfies the following property: for a fixed <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{y}},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{y}},i)</annotation></semantics></math>, sample <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> from this posterior distribution <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pi(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}},i)</annotation></semantics></math>, and then the desired <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>’s per-position posterior follows <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="v\sim p(x^{i}=\cdot\,|\,y\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\sim p(x^{i}=\cdot\,|\,y\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math>. This nice property holds since the sampling process of <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i))</annotation></semantics></math> forms a Markov chain–<math id="A2.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is conditioned on <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{y}},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{y}},i)</annotation></semantics></math> is conditioned on <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">By using this property, we now complete the proof; the remaining parts are the same as Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (PRISM). ‣ 3.1 Theoretical foundation of PRISM ‣ 3 PRISM ‣ Fine-Tuning Masked Diffusion for ProvableSelf-Correction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\phi)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\phi)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex21.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[{\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}}\,)}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>BCE</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[{\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}}\,)}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex22.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{y,i}\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{S}\sim\Pi(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}},i)}\mathbb{E}_{v\sim p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}}),{\mathbf{z}}={\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\mathcal{S}}}\left[\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[v={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>BCE</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{y,i}\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{S}\sim\Pi(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}},i)}\mathbb{E}_{v\sim p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}=\cdot\,|\,{\mathbf{z}}),{\mathbf{z}}={\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\mathcal{S}}}\left[\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[v={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\theta}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex23.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{y,i}\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{S}\sim\Pi(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[-{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q}\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})-({\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}1-q})\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}1-g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">q</mi><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">q</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">−</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{y,i}\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{S}\sim\Pi(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[-{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q}\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})-({\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}1-q})\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}1-g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q}=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\mid{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q}=p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\mid{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the expectation over <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{S}\sim\Pi(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\sim\Pi(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}},i)</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="A2.SS3.p6.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">absorbs</em> into <math id="A2.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q}</annotation></semantics></math>, finally derived to our desired form;</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex24.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[{\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}}\,)}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{y,i}\left[-{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q^{\prime}}\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})-({\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}1-q^{\prime}})\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}1-g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>BCE</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">′</mo></msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">−</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">−</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">ϕ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#009F86;" mathcolor="#009F86" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{z}},({\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[{\mathrm{BCE}(\,\mathbf{1}[{\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}]\,,\,{{\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})}}\,)}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{y,i}\left[-{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q^{\prime}}\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})-({\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}1-q^{\prime}})\log({\color[rgb]{0,0.625,0.5273}1-g_{\phi}^{i}({\mathbf{y}})})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">q</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#623E99;" mathcolor="#623E99">′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\color[rgb]{0.3828,0.2422,0.6016}q^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex25.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{S}\sim\Pi(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\mid{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\mathcal{S}})\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{S}\sim\Pi(\cdot\mid{\mathbf{y}},i)}\left[p({\mathbf{x}}^{i}={\mathbf{y}}^{i}\mid{\mathbf{y}}\oplus\mathbf{m}_{\mathcal{S}})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
